WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:11.300
In this last video, you add the final touches to the scene by importing the missing elements. These include people, cars, and some additional props.

2
00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:16.679
Import the last Datasmith file, the one named Props, as you have learned before.

3
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.699
There will be some issues with the import that you can check in the message log.

4
00:00:28.519 --> 00:00:35.219
These mostly relate to animated people and animated cars that do not translate well between Twinmotion and Unreal Engine.

5
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:41.960
Delete the redundant background and get closer to the scene.

6
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:51.609
Note that any posed characters imported correctly, but that any animated characters did not.

7
00:00:52.299 --> 00:00:55.100
That's the import restriction as it currently stands.

8
00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:01.710
Anything else that is static, like furniture and decorations, imported flawlessly.

9
00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:09.620
Interestingly, all vehicles did import, but they are static. Their animations didn't come through.

10
00:01:10.349 --> 00:01:13.549
Again, this is a known restriction that we currently have.

11
00:01:14.439 --> 00:01:22.629
However, note the green squares in front and behind each car. These are decal objects for the headlights and taillights.

12
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:29.930
Unfortunately, their material did not translate correctly between Twinmotion and Unreal Engine, so we'll need to fix that.

13
00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:41.280
Create a new folder under Content Theatron, Props, Materials, and name it Decals.

14
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.019
Create a new material named MCarLights.

15
00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:54.629
Set the new material to be used as a deferred decal and set the blend mode to Translucent.

16
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:11.490
Look in the Textures folder and note the two imported textures TLightMaskFrontBC and TLightMaskRearBC.

17
00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:14.960
These represent the front and taillights on the cars.

18
00:02:15.939 --> 00:02:21.740
Drag the TLightMaskFrontBC texture into the newly created material.

19
00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.669
Convert the node to a parameter and call it Light Mask.

20
00:02:27.969 --> 00:02:30.370
This will allow you to change it at the instance level.

21
00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:36.930
You could wire it directly to the emissive channel, but let's give ourselves a bit more control.

22
00:02:37.979 --> 00:02:42.280
Press 3 and click in the material editor to add a color node.

23
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.400
Again, make it a parameter and name it Tint.

24
00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:53.370
You'll use this to tint the mask which will be useful for the taillights.

25
00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.669
Make the default color white though.

26
00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.169
Press M and click to add a Multiply node.

27
00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:13.590
Multiply the two nodes and then add another Multiply node before you wire that to the emissive channel.

28
00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.849
The second Multiply node will be useful to control the intensity of the effect.

29
00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.250
Add a Scalar node, S on the keyboard, and name it Intensity.

30
00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:31.030
Wire it to the second Multiply node and set its default value to 1.

31
00:03:33.449 --> 00:03:36.449
Save the material and close the material editor.

32
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:48.310
Create two material instances named miHeadlights and miTaillights.

33
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.759
Adjust their parameters such as intensities and colors.

34
00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.349
You may need to revisit these values again.

35
00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.449
Also make sure the taillights are based on the appropriate mask image.

36
00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:19.509
Use the Outline Research Box to filter all the Decal F actors in the scene.

37
00:04:20.819 --> 00:04:23.920
Note that the Twinmotion material assignment didn't carry through.

38
00:04:25.189 --> 00:04:28.389
Assign the miHeadlights material you just created.

39
00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.079
And repeat the procedure for the taillights or Decal R.

40
00:04:44.019 --> 00:04:46.120
Switch to Nighttime to view the effects.

41
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.199
Lastly, you can help the night scene by adding a bit of a moon effect.

42
00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:01.040
Add a Directional Light to the scene.

43
00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.779
It will conflict with the SunSky system.

44
00:05:09.399 --> 00:05:12.800
Rename the actor Moonlight and move it to the Lighting folder.

45
00:05:19.779 --> 00:05:23.980
Note the error in the viewport. You need to adjust the Forward Shading Priority value.

46
00:05:25.339 --> 00:05:32.149
Search for it in the Details panel and set it to 1 to remove the conflict.

47
00:05:34.379 --> 00:05:42.230
Look also for Atmosphere and set the Sunlight Index to 1.

48
00:05:43.279 --> 00:05:45.379
This will bring the sky and clouds into effect.

49
00:05:47.069 --> 00:05:55.540
Change the light color to a blue that you like and reduce the intensity.

50
00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:08.250
This will make the environment a bit more visible at night instead of being drowned in darkness.

51
00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:17.149
It will not affect the scene much in daytime.

52
00:06:26.579 --> 00:06:30.879
Save your project now that you have transferred your Twinmotion scene into UE5.

53
00:06:32.949 --> 00:06:37.050
This brings us to the end of this class. I hope you have found it useful.
